 RISCV Debug System Overview
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V Debug Specification defines a modular and transport-agnostic
 framework for external debuggers to interact with RISC-V processors.
 Its architecture separates transport, control, and execution layers to
 provide scalable and flexible debug support.
 The primary components include the Debug Transport Module (DTM), the Debug
 Module Interface (DMI), and the Debug Module (DM), each fulfilling distinct
 roles in the debug pathway.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Debug communication typically begins with a debugger host (e.g., GDB) interfacing
 with a hardware driver (e.g., OpenOCD), which interacts with the Debug Transport
 Module (e.g., JTAG or UART).
 The DTM then relays these instructions to the Debug Module via the DMI,
 which exposes control/status registers and enables register-level operations
 on the processor core (hart).
 This architecture supports both single and multi-hart environments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug System Components
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V debug architecture is modular, consisting of three primary components
: the Debug Transport Module (DTM), the Debug Module Interface (DMI), and
 the Debug Module (DM).
 These components work in conjunction to facilitate interaction between
 an external debugger and the internal state of one or more RISC-V harts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Transport Module (DTM):
\series default
 
\emph default
Acts as the physical access point for an external debugger.
 It implements a protocol-specific interface—typically JTAG or Serial Wire
 Debug (SWD)—and forwards incoming debug transactions to the Debug Module
 through the DMI.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Module Interface (DMI):
\emph default
 
\series default
Serves as a memory-mapped interface between the DTM and the Debug Module.
 It supports 32-bit read and write transactions to a defined address space,
 enabling command and status exchange.
 The interface is intentionally minimalistic to ensure lightweight integration
 with transport mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Debug Module (DM):
\series default
\emph default
 Implements the primary debugging functionality.
 It controls hart execution (halt, resume), provides abstract access to
 memory and registers, and supports programmable instruction execution using
 a program buffer.
 A single DM typically serves all harts in a system, allowing fine-grained
 control via internal hart selection mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each hart (hardware thread) is associated with one DM.
 In multi-hart systems, a centralized DM facilitates debugging across all
 harts, simplifying design and reducing overhead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The DMI provides the communication pathway between the JTAG interface (via
 the DTM) and the internal Debug Module.
 It exposes a 32-bit addressable register space where debug operations can
 be initiated or queried.
 The interface is streamlined, requiring only address and data lines, enabling
 efficient read/write access without additional control handshaking.
 This simplicity supports both hardware and software implementations with
 minimal complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Debug Module exposes several memory-mapped registers via DMI to configure,
 control, and monitor debugging operations.
 Key registers include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmcontrol (0x10): 
\series default
\emph default
Enables the debug module, selects the target hart, and issues halt/resume
 requests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmstatus (0x11):
\series default
\emph default
 Reflects the current status of all managed harts (e.g., halted, running,
 unavailable).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
abstractcs (0x16):
\series default
 
\emph default
Indicates the availability and configuration of abstract command mechanisms,
 such as the program buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
command (0x17):
\series default
\emph default
 Encodes abstract commands to be executed by the DM (e.g., register access).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
abstractauto (0x18):
\emph default
 
\series default
Automates execution of abstract commands and data access patterns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
data0–dataN (0x04–0x0f):
\emph default
 
\series default
Temporary storage registers for abstract command operands or results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
progbuf0–N (0x20–0x2X):
\emph default
 
\series default
Holds RISC-V instructions to be executed during debug mode using the program
 buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additional registers such as hartinfo, nextdm, dmcs2, and implementation-specifi
c extensions can be included to support more advanced functionality or multicore
 management.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Abstract Commands and Program Buffer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Abstract commands provide a flexible, implementation-independent method
 for executing fundamental debug tasks.
 The DM supports the following command types:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access Register: 
\series default
\emph default
Read or write general-purpose registers (GPRs) or control/status registers
 (CSRs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Quick Access:
\series default
\emph default
 Perform a rapid sequence of halt, instruction execution, and resume for
 common diagnostics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access Memory:
\series default
\emph default
 Read or write system memory from the perspective of the target hart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For more complex operations, a Program Buffer allows execution of short
 RISC-V instruction sequences.
 The buffer typically supports at least two instruction slots and must terminate
 with an ebreak (or c.ebreak) instruction.
 This mechanism allows custom register transfers, CSR manipulation, and
 even limited branching within debug mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hart Debug Control & CSRs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each hart features dedicated control and status registers for managing debug
 state:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dcsr (0x7b0): 
\series default
\emph default
The Debug Control and Status Register includes fields indicating the cause
 of entry into debug mode, the current privilege level, single-step enable,
 and other configuration bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dpc (0x7b1):
\series default
\emph default
 Debug Program Counter stores the address at which the hart was halted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dscratch0, dscratch1 (0x7b2, 0x7b3):
\series default
 
\emph default
General-purpose scratch registers available for temporary storage by the
 debugger during execution of abstract commands or program buffer instructions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These CSRs ensure safe and controlled operation of the debugger and allow
 seamless entry and exit from debug mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Mode Operation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A RISC-V hart enters Debug Mode when specific events or conditions occur
 that indicate external or internal debug intervention.
 These conditions include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
External Halt Request:
\series default
\emph default
 The debugger sets the haltreq bit in the dmcontrol register via the Debug
 Module Interface (DMI).
 This signal requests that the hart halt at the next instruction boundary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Trigger Events:
\emph default
 
\series default
The hart is configured with hardware breakpoints or watchpoints (via trigger
 modules).
 Upon matching a trigger condition—such as a specific instruction fetch,
 memory access, or address match—the hart automatically transitions to Debug
 Mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Single-Step Execution:
\series default
\emph default
 If the step bit is set in the dcsr (Debug Control and Status Register),
 the hart executes one instruction and then automatically halts and enters
 Debug Mode.
 This is useful for instruction-level stepping during software debugging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once a hart enters Debug Mode, the following changes occur in its execution
 environment:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Instruction Fetch Is Disabled:
\series default
\emph default
 The hart's normal instruction execution pipeline is stalled.
 It does not fetch or retire instructions from the normal program memory,
 effectively freezing the program state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Access to Debug Resources:
\series default
\emph default
 While halted, the Debug Module gains exclusive access to the hart’s internal
 state.
 Abstract commands issued by writing to the command register allow the debugger
 to read or write general-purpose registers (GPRs), control and status registers
 (CSRs), or memory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Execution via Program Buffer:
\series default
\emph default
 A small, fixed-size instruction buffer (progbuf) inside the Debug Module
 allows the debugger to inject and execute RISC-V instructions while the
 hart is halted.
 The program buffer enables flexible, direct manipulation of the hart state
 (e.g., loading registers from memory, executing computation, etc.).
 For correct behavior, program buffer execution must end with an ebreak
 or c.ebreak instruction, which causes control to return to the Debug Module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Preservation of Program State:
\series default
\emph default
 Upon entering Debug Mode, the current value of the Program Counter (PC)
 is saved in the dpc register.
 The cause of entry is recorded in dcsr.cause, which can indicate external
 request, trigger match, single-step, or exception during debug execution.
 The dcsr also reflects the previous privilege level and various control
 bits such as step, ebreakm, ebreaks, and ebreaku.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Exiting Debug Mode is initiated by the debugger through the dmcontrol register.
 The debugger must clear the haltreq bit and set the resumereq bit.
 Once the Debug Module confirms that the hart is ready (via dmstatus), the
 hart resumes normal instruction execution from the address stored in dpc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If an invalid instruction is executed in the program buffer, or if an exception
 occurs during abstract command execution, the hart remains in Debug Mode,
 and the corresponding error code is updated in the cmderr field of the
 abstractcs register.
 This ensures that faulty debug operations do not unintentionally resume
 program execution or corrupt state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This robust mechanism for entering, managing, and exiting Debug Mode makes
 the RISC-V debug architecture suitable for a wide range of use cases—from
 deeply embedded systems requiring minimal overhead to complex multi-core
 SoCs needing advanced debugging and trace capabilities.
\end_layout

