\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the above study report we have compared various industrial and academic
 RISC-V designs.
 RISC-V ISA has been studied in order to understand the software-hardware
 interaction and how hardware can be better designed to address anomalies
 such as hazards and stalls at the hardware level, thereby reducing compiler
 dependencies and accelerating software development.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then proposed our own RISC-V architecture, incorporating design blocks
 and strategies informed by prior literature.
 To address data hazards, our processor employs bypassing/forwarding techniques
 and a 2-bit branch prediction mechanism, a commonly adopted method across
 academic designs.
 The processor was implemented in Chisel, enabling modular and efficient
 development of components like memory and cache, which would have been
 more laborious in Verilog.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, we developed a RISC-V compliant debugger based on the official
 debug specification.
 The debugger integrates a JTAG-based Debug Transport Module (DTM), a Debug
 Module Interface (DMI), and a Debug Module (DM), supporting core features
 such as halt/resume, register and CSR access, and abstract command execution
 via a program buffer.
 This ensures real-time control and observability of the processor during
 both simulation and hardware execution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Together, the processor and debugger form a complete platform that is well-suite
d for educational use, research exploration, and embedded system prototyping.
\end_layout

